+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report — `byte-unit`
+
+Property-based tests were added with [hegel](https://hegel.dev) (`hegeltest` 0.28.2, added
+with `cargo add --dev hegeltest --ignore-rust-version`; hegeltest's MSRV is 1.86 while the
+crate pins `rust-version = "1.85"` — a maintainer decision if these tests are upstreamed).
+Tests live in the existing integration test files `tests/byte.rs` (module
+`hegel_properties`) and `tests/unit.rs` (module `hegel_properties`), and run under the
+default feature set (`std` + `byte`, i.e. a `u64`-backed `Byte`). Generators and constants
+are written to stay correct under the `u128` feature as well (cfg-gated `MAX_BYTES`,
+`MAX_PRECISION`, unit lists).
+
+## Properties tested
+
+All properties draw `Byte` values from a composite generator (`arb_byte`) covering the
+full representable range, boosted toward boundaries (`0`, `1`, `MAX`) and toward exact
+multiples of every unit.
+
+### `tests/byte.rs`
+
+1. **`byte_parse_str_never_panics`** — `Byte::parse_str` never panics, on arbitrary
+   Unicode text and on near-miss numeric strings (`"<u64><junk>"` with junk like `"."`,
+   `"-"`, `"e3"`, `".."`).
+   *Evidence:* parsers must be total on `&str` (skill Tier-1 "parse robustness"); the
+   crate's own tests feed it malformed inputs expecting `Err`, never a panic.
+2. **`display_reparses_to_same_byte`** — `Byte::parse_str(byte.to_string(), _) == byte`
+   over the full range (including `u64::MAX`, exercising the parse path's `Decimal`
+   accumulation with 20-digit integers).
+   *Evidence:* the existing `parse_str` unit test includes
+   `("18446744073709551615", Byte::from_u64(u64::MAX))`; `Display` docs show plain output
+   is the byte count.
+3. **`alternate_display_reparses_to_same_byte`** — every documented alternate format style
+   (`{:#}`, `{:#.p}`, `{:-#}`, `{:>+#15.p}`, `{:#15}`, precision 0..=19) re-parses to the
+   identical `Byte`.
+   *Evidence:* alternate `Display` is documented "with an exact unit ... still precisely"
+   and is implemented on top of `get_recoverable_unit`.
+4. **`recoverable_unit_recovers_byte_exactly`** —
+   `Byte::from_decimal_with_unit(byte.get_recoverable_unit(allow_in_bits, precision)) == byte`
+   for both `allow_in_bits` values and all documented precisions (0..=19; 0..=26 under
+   `u128`).
+   *Evidence:* doc contract "Find the appropriate unit and value that can be used to
+   recover back to this `Byte` precisely."
+5. **`exact_unit_recovers_byte_exactly`** — `get_exact_unit` returns `(n, unit)` with
+   `n * unit_bytes == byte` exactly and `Byte::from_u128_with_unit(n, unit) == byte`,
+   both `allow_in_bits` values. Unit sizes come from the public `as_bits_u128() >> 3`,
+   not the private helper.
+   *Evidence:* doc contract "the largest unit which is the greatest factor".
+6. **`from_u64_with_unit_matches_u128_arithmetic`** — for every unit and full-range
+   `u64` sizes (boosted at 0/1/MAX−1/MAX), `from_u64_with_unit` agrees with independent
+   `u128` arithmetic: `ceil(n/8)` for `Bit`, `n * unit_bytes` otherwise, and returns
+   `None` exactly when the product exceeds the representable maximum.
+   *Evidence:* doc contract per constructor ("If the calculated byte is too large, this
+   function will return `None`"; "If the input unit is `Bit`, the calculated byte will be
+   rounded up").
+7. **`parse_str_matches_decimal_oracle`** — parsing constructed
+   `"<int>[.<frac>]<0-2 spaces><unit-or-empty>"` strings agrees with an independent
+   oracle: `rust_decimal`'s own string parser (`Decimal::from_str`) composed with
+   `from_decimal_with_unit`. The hand-rolled digit/fraction/whitespace loop in
+   `Byte::parse_str` is independent code from `Decimal::from_str`.
+   *Evidence:* `parse_str` docs list the accepted grammar; existing unit tests encode
+   individual instances (`"1.2kb"`, `"76.65 MB"`, `"2 kiB"`).
+8. **`appropriate_unit_selects_largest_fitting_unit`** — `get_appropriate_unit` picks a
+   unit of the requested `UnitType` that is the largest one not exceeding the byte count
+   (and `B` when nothing fits), checked against a spec-restated ascending unit ladder.
+   *Evidence:* method name/docs ("Find the appropriate unit") plus the `UnitType` docs.
+9. **`appropriate_unit_get_byte_is_close`** — round-tripping through
+   `get_appropriate_unit(..).get_byte()` is within f64 rounding error of the original:
+   `|back − byte| ≤ (byte >> 47) + 1` (worst-case relative error of the
+   f64→Decimal→ceil pipeline is ~2⁻⁵¹; the bound gives 16× headroom).
+   *Evidence:* `get_byte` docs ("The result may not be logically equal to the original
+   `Byte` instance due to the accuracy of floating-point numbers") — inexact, but the
+   error must be floating-point-sized, not unit-sized. *Excludes* the known-bug region
+   near the maximum (see below), with a comment referencing the canonical failing test.
+10. **`adjusted_byte_ordering_is_monotone`** — `AdjustedByte`'s `Ord` (defined via
+    `get_byte`) orders two values adjusted to the same (arbitrary) unit consistently
+    with their byte counts: `a <= b ⇒ a.get_adjusted_unit(u) <= b.get_adjusted_unit(u)`.
+    *Evidence:* `PartialEq`/`Ord` impls in `src/byte/adjusted/mod.rs` compare via
+    `get_byte`. Same known-bug region excluded.
+11. **`known_bug_adjusted_byte_get_byte_panics_near_max`** — KNOWN FAILURE pin (see
+    Suspected bugs). Deterministic: the generator is pinned to
+    `[u64::MAX − 512, u64::MAX]`, where every input panics. `#[cfg(not(feature = "u128"))]`.
+
+### `tests/unit.rs`
+
+12. **`unit_as_str_parse_roundtrip`** — for every `Unit`,
+    `Unit::parse_str(unit.as_str(), false, prefer_byte) == unit` for both `prefer_byte`
+    values (canonical strings are explicit about bit vs byte, so `prefer_byte` must not
+    matter).
+    *Evidence:* the existing `parse_str` unit test's table encodes many instances of
+    exactly this.
+13. **`unit_parse_str_never_panics`** — `Unit::parse_str` never panics on arbitrary text
+    with any flag combination. (The byte-scanning + `get_char_from_bytes` unsafe path
+    makes this worth having: multi-byte UTF-8 error characters are reconstructed with
+    `from_utf8_unchecked`.)
+
+## Test results
+
+- **Liveness check:** temporarily inverted the assertion in
+  `display_reparses_to_same_byte`; hegel reported a shrunk minimal counterexample
+  (`Byte(0)`). Reverted, and the `.hegel/` failure database was deleted afterwards.
+- **Default run (`cargo test --tests`):** 12 of 13 properties pass; the KNOWN FAILURE
+  pin fails deterministically (shrinks to `v = 18446744073709551103`, i.e.
+  `u64::MAX − 512`), alongside all 6 pre-existing unit tests passing.
+- **Exploratory 10× run (`test_cases = 1000` on every property, then reverted):** no
+  additional failures.
+- `.hegel/` added to `.gitignore`.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+**`AdjustedByte::get_byte` (and therefore `AdjustedByte`'s `PartialEq`/`Ord`/`Eq`)
+panics for byte counts near the representable maximum.**
+
+- Reproducer: `Byte::from_u64(u64::MAX).get_appropriate_unit(UnitType::Binary).get_byte()`
+  → `called 'Option::unwrap()' on a 'None' value` at `src/byte/adjusted/mod.rs:224`.
+- Mechanism: `get_appropriate_unit`/`get_adjusted_unit` compute `value` as an `f64`
+  division, which can round *up* — every byte count within 512 of `u64::MAX` becomes
+  exactly `16.0 EiB` = 2⁶⁴ bytes. `get_byte` then does
+  `Byte::from_f64_with_unit(self.value, self.unit).unwrap()`, and
+  `from_f64_with_unit` correctly returns `None` for 2⁶⁴ > `u64::MAX`, so the `unwrap`
+  panics. `UnitType::Decimal` also panics for `u64::MAX` (value rounds to
+  `18.446744073709552 EB` > `u64::MAX` bytes); some nearby values (e.g.
+  `15.999999999999998 EiB`) also fail via the `Decimal` multiplication path.
+- Blast radius: any comparison (`==`, `<`, sorting) of `AdjustedByte` values in this
+  region panics too, since `PartialEq`/`Ord` call `get_byte`.
+- The docs only warn the result "may not be logically equal ... due to the accuracy of
+  floating-point numbers" — a panic is not covered by that caveat.
+- Pinned by `known_bug_adjusted_byte_get_byte_panics_near_max` (fails deterministically
+  on every run). Sibling properties (9, 10) exclude the region via `GET_BYTE_SAFE_MAX`
+  with comments referencing the canonical test; the bound protects the tests from the
+  known bug, not the library's contract. Not fixed, per instructions.
+
+The analogous region under the `u128` feature (near 10²⁷ − 1) is expected to have the
+same problem but was not exercised (default features only); the pin test is cfg-gated to
+`not(feature = "u128")`.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **Exact `AdjustedByte` round-trip** (`adjusted.get_byte() == byte`) — explicitly
+  disclaimed by the `get_byte` docs; replaced with the bounded-error property (9).
+- **`AdjustedByte` equality across units for the same `Byte`**
+  (`b.get_adjusted_unit(u1) == b.get_adjusted_unit(u2)`) — unsound for the same reason:
+  `get_byte` reconstruction error differs per unit, so logical equality is not promised.
+- **Unit round-trip with `ignore_case = true`** — deliberately not asserted:
+  `ignore_case = true` maps bit-unit canonical strings to byte units by design
+  (`"Kib"` → `KiB`), so the round-trip only holds for `ignore_case = false` (property 12).
+- **Maximality of `get_exact_unit`'s choice** ("the *largest* unit ...") — dropped: a
+  faithful oracle would re-implement the crate-private ordered multiples table in the
+  test; only the exactness/inverse half (property 5) is asserted.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- The Rust reference's Setup section was accurate and saved time twice: the MSRV
+  conflict (`--ignore-rust-version` retry) and the package-vs-crate naming
+  (`hegeltest` vs `use hegel::`).
+- The "no global case-count override" gotcha (#13) is real; a `sed` over
+  `#[hegel::test]` worked fine for the 10× pass but an env-var override would be nicer.
+- The "keep a failing test that pins a real bug deterministically" guidance worked
+  exactly as described: pinning the generator to the failing region
+  (`min_value(u64::MAX - 512)`) makes the failure reproduce on every run and shrink to
+  the region's minimum.
+- The skill's "forgot about zero / precision-through-f64" bug-class hints pointed
+  directly at the one real bug found (f64 rounding at the top of the range rather than
+  at zero, but the same class).
+- One friction point: `sampled_from` requiring `T: Send + Sync + 'static` was satisfied
+  here (plain enums), but `one_of!` branches with differently-typed generators unified
+  only because all branches were the same generator type; the reference's note about
+  `.boxed()` covered the fallback so this never blocked.
